module gunbc.rung_drop.dashboard_merge_ready_semantic_admissibility

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.rung_drop { RungDrop, Standing }

data dashboard_merge_ready_semantic_admissibility: RungDrop = RungDrop {
  identity: "dashboard_merge_ready_semantic_admissibility" as NonEmptyStr,
  subject: "Dashboard MergeReady omits repository-semantic admissibility",
  declared: "2026-09-01",
  standing: Standing,
  authored: "**THE DASHBOARD REPORTS `MergeReady` WITHOUT CONSUMING WHETHER THE CHANGE IS SEMANTICALLY ADMISSIBLE TO ITS REPOSITORY (2026-09-01).** The dashboard's `pr_merge_ready_digest.mjs` `meetsMergeCriteria` constructs the verdict from review-vote tallies, absence of unresolved or request-changes reviews, GitHub's conflict-free `mergeable` observation, and a non-failing check summary. Those are real mechanical facts, but they do not answer the separate repository-semantic question required by the working agreement: whether the diff actually satisfies its request and the repository's authority documents. An approval explicitly means only that its reviewer found no blocking defect; it is not evidence that the requested change was delivered. Calling their join `MergeReady` therefore turns an incomplete projection into an affirmative operational instruction and can page an operator to land a mechanically clean change that misses its request. EXECUTED SPECIMEN, observed 2026-09-01 with gunbc#9932 at `cc1f3ff60` against main at `6bdc9492c2`: `meetsMergeCriteria` emitted `ready=true` while the decision-time instrument `git merge-tree --write-tree origin/main cc1f3ff60` returned `rc=1`. Both are re-derivable at those two pinned subjects: the driver verdict deterministically, and the dashboard verdict by replaying its named predicate over the same head. The review tallies and check summary that fed that verdict are deliberately NOT recorded here — a vote count on a head is a mutable population with no producer that re-derives it, and copying one would be the transcription this document forbids. The generated-artifact driver refuses `GeneratedArtifactConcurrentDivergence` when both sides changed a generated projection since the merge base; GitHub's plain text-merge verdict never invokes that driver, so its merge can succeed with no conflict, marker or signal while yielding bytes that project neither side's authorities. The repository-semantic answer was Refused while every fact the dashboard consumes admitted the PR. Naming both immutable subjects and the producer makes this the gap firing in production, not a conclusion transcribed from a moving PR. PREVIOUS RUNG: mitigatable — the worker or manager had to reread the request and authority documents before declaring readiness, so the missing semantic fact was bounded only by human diligence and was never a mechanical guarantee. TEMPORARY RUNG: below the ladder on the dashboard-verdict path — no `RepositoryMergeAdmissibility` observation enters the constructor, no typed unknown or refusal is carried, and the affirmative verdict is indistinguishable from one whose semantic admission was actually decided. The review, CI and conflict paths retain their own mechanically preventable rungs; this row does not lower or relabel them. REASON: the dashboard automated the mechanical half of the decision and named the projection after the whole decision without first modeling the other half. BOUNDED POPULATION: every `merge_ready` digest entry constructed by `meetsMergeCriteria`, including its stale-entry revalidation through the same predicate; parent and manager notifications are projections of those entries and are not separate instances. OUTSIDE-THE-MODEL BOUNDARY: semantic admissibility may ultimately require judgment, but that does not license fabricating `Admissible`; an undecided judgment is a first-class standing and must block the affirmative verdict. RESTORATION TRIGGER: in `gunb-ai/ctrl`, a typed `RepositoryMergeAdmissibility` input with at least admitted, refused and undecided standings is consumed by the one canonical merge-readiness predicate, and both initial construction and stale revalidation refuse to emit `MergeReady` unless the exact repository-and-head subject is `Admitted`. A boolean inferred from approvals, CI, GitHub `mergeable`, session state, or the absence of a recorded objection does not satisfy the trigger, because each merely renames a mechanical fact already present. The discriminating RED is an otherwise mechanically ready PR whose semantic standing is undecided or refused; the positive control is the same mechanical packet with an exact-head admitted receipt. The repair belongs to `gunb-ai/ctrl`, which owns the dashboard constructor; this repository records the rung honestly and does not mint a second merge-admission authority."
}
